  "rationale": "The spec requires >= 1 MSPS throughput, which rules out iterative and unrolled_k (they produce one result every several cycles, so throughput = Fmax/cycles_per_result; even at high Fmax, e.g. 200 MHz / 13 cycles ~ 15 MSPS is fine, but the area is dominated by the shared datapath and the constraint is easily met). However, the primary objective is minimising luts_plus_ffs, and pipelined_m with m>1 offers the best area/throughput trade-off. I focus 100% of the first round on pipelined and pipelined_m because they are the only families that guarantee 1 result/cycle throughput independent of Fmax, making them the natural candidates for the Pareto front. W=12..16 and N=10..14 are chosen because: (1) max_abs_err <= 2^-10 requires sufficient precision; with W=12, N=10, truncation, the error is typically around 2^-10 to 2^-11, so this range brackets the feasibility boundary; (2) angle_guard 0..2 and frac_guard 0..2 explore the accuracy/area trade-off without wasting budget on extreme values that either blow up area or are unnecessary. Rounding is included as a categorical choice since it affects error at no significant area cost. The 50/50 split between pipelined and pipelined_m ensures we map both ends of the area trade-off: pipelined anchors the high-area/high-timing end, pipelined_m anchors the low-area end. This should reveal the Pareto front shape in round 1, allowing round 2 to refine ranges or add iterative/unrolled_k if they turn out to be competitive on area (they have fewer FFs but lower throughput, so they may still meet 1 MSPS if Fmax is high enough)."
